      "source": [
        "# Exercises XP: Day 3 - BERT in Practice\n",
        "Completed notebook with code and written explanations for each exercise.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "bbae25ca",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "bbae25ca"
      },
      "source": [
        "## What you'll learn\n",
        "- How to tokenize text with BERT and understand special tokens.\n",
        "- How to run a pretrained sentiment pipeline.\n",
        "- How to build custom BERT-based sentiment and NER analyzers.\n",
        "- How to compare encoder (BERT) versus decoder (GPT) families.\n",
        "- How BERT supplies retrieval power inside a RAG stack.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "c2aa5bf0",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "c2aa5bf0"
      },
      "source": [
        "## What you will create\n",
        "- A fully tokenized sentence with visible IDs and special tokens.\n",
        "- A working sentiment pipeline powered by a fine-tuned DistilBERT model.\n",
        "- Custom helper classes for sentiment classification and NER.\n",
        "- A comparison table that contrasts BERT and GPT.\n",
        "- A written explanation of how BERT embeddings drive retrieval in RAG.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "5ed16a45",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "5ed16a45"
      },
      "source": [
        "> Mandatory preparation: watch \"PyTorch in 100 Seconds\" so the tensor outputs below feel intuitive."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "4304ecfe",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "4304ecfe"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Exercise 1 - Tokenization with BERT\n",
        "Objective: Explore how the bert-base-uncased tokenizer prepares text for model input.\n",
        "\n",
        "Instructions:\n",
        "1. (Optional) Install the required libraries.\n",
        "2. Load the tokenizer, craft a sample sentence, and encode it with padding plus truncation.\n",
        "3. Print the tokens next to their integer IDs and flag the special tokens.\n",
        "4. Inspect the attention mask to see how padding is hidden from the model.\n",
        "\n",
        "Deliverables:\n",
        "- The code below prints the tokens and IDs, with BERT special tokens marked in a separate column.\n",
        "- I use `max_length=24`, which leaves enough room for the sample sentence, `[CLS]`, `[SEP]`, and several `[PAD]` tokens so the attention mask is easy to inspect.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "950dde59",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "950dde59"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Optional setup: install dependencies if they are missing in your environment.\n",
        "# %pip install -q transformers torch\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "d631bc25",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "d631bc25"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "from transformers import AutoTokenizer\n",
        "\n",
        "tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"bert-base-uncased\")\n",
        "\n",
        "sample_sentence = \"BERT helps students understand language models quickly.\"\n",
        "print(sample_sentence)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "3da3ac84",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "3da3ac84"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "encoding = tokenizer(\n",
        "    sample_sentence,\n",
        "    add_special_tokens=True,\n",
        "    padding=\"max_length\",\n",
        "    truncation=True,\n",
        "    max_length=24,  # Enough room for this sentence plus [CLS], [SEP], and [PAD] tokens.\n",
        "    return_attention_mask=True,\n",
        "    return_tensors=\"pt\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "input_ids = encoding[\"input_ids\"][0].tolist()\n",
        "tokens = tokenizer.convert_ids_to_tokens(input_ids)\n",
        "print(\"index | token        | id    | special\")\n",
        "print(\"--------------------------------------\")\n",
        "for idx, (token, token_id) in enumerate(zip(tokens, input_ids)):\n",
        "    special = \"YES\" if token in tokenizer.all_special_tokens else \"\"\n",
        "    print(f\"{idx:>5} | {token:<12} | {token_id:>5} | {special}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\nAttention mask:\", encoding[\"attention_mask\"][0].tolist())\n",
        "special_positions = [(i, tok) for i, tok in enumerate(tokens) if tok in tokenizer.all_special_tokens]\n",
        "print(\"Special tokens (index, token):\", special_positions)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "a19d64b0",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "a19d64b0"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Exercise 1 reflection\n",
        "- `[CLS]` is added at the start of the sequence. In BERT-style classification tasks, the final hidden state for this position is often used as a summary representation of the full input.\n",
        "- `[SEP]` marks the end of the sentence. For sentence-pair tasks it also separates the first sentence from the second one.\n",
        "- The attention mask uses `1` for real tokens and `0` for padded positions. This tells the model to ignore `[PAD]` tokens during attention so padding does not affect the meaning of the sentence.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "8acfa095",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "8acfa095"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Exercise 2 - Sentiment analysis pipeline\n",
        "Objective: Use a pretrained DistilBERT sentiment pipeline to classify a sentence.\n",
        "\n",
        "Instructions:\n",
        "1. Import the `pipeline` helper from transformers.\n",
        "2. Build a pipeline that loads `distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english`.\n",
        "3. Pass in a sentence and review the predicted label and score.\n",
        "\n",
        "Deliverables:\n",
        "- Tested sentence: `I really enjoyed this lesson because the examples made BERT feel practical.`\n",
        "- The code below returns the predicted label and confidence score, then prints them in a readable format.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "e0418b34",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "e0418b34"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "from transformers import pipeline\n",
        "\n",
        "sentiment_pipeline = pipeline(\n",
        "    task=\"sentiment-analysis\",\n",
        "    model=\"distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "sentence = \"I really enjoyed this lesson because the examples made BERT feel practical.\"\n",
        "prediction = sentiment_pipeline(sentence)\n",
        "print(f\"Sentence: {sentence}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Label: {prediction[0]['label']}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Confidence score: {prediction[0]['score']:.4f}\")\n",
        "prediction\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "d0caf203",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "d0caf203"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Exercise 2 reflection\n",
        "- The sentence is clearly positive because words like `enjoyed` and `practical` express approval, so a `POSITIVE` prediction matches my expectation.\n",
        "- The confidence score is the model's estimated probability for the selected label. A score close to `1.0` means the model is very confident; a score closer to `0.5` would mean the decision is more uncertain.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "e7317d74",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "e7317d74"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Exercise 3 - Custom sentiment analyzer class\n",
        "Objective: Rebuild the pipeline manually so you control tokenization, tensors, and scoring.\n",
        "\n",
        "Instructions:\n",
        "1. Import `AutoTokenizer` and `AutoModelForSequenceClassification`.\n",
        "2. Implement `BERTSentimentAnalyzer` with methods for initialization, preprocessing, and prediction.\n",
        "3. Test the class with multiple sentences.\n",
        "\n",
        "Hints:\n",
        "- Keep a `max_length` attribute so you can reuse it while tokenizing.\n",
        "- Apply `torch.softmax` to transform logits into probabilities.\n",
        "- Return both the label and the probability for clarity.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "7fc452e7",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "7fc452e7"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "import torch\n",
        "from transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForSequenceClassification\n",
        "from typing import Any, Dict\n",
        "\n",
        "class BERTSentimentAnalyzer:\n",
        "    def __init__(self, model_name: str = \"distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english\", max_length: int = 128):\n",
        "        self.model_name = model_name\n",
        "        self.max_length = max_length\n",
        "        self.device = torch.device(\"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\")\n",
        "        self.tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_name)\n",
        "        self.model = AutoModelForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained(model_name).to(self.device)\n",
        "        self.model.eval()\n",
        "\n",
        "    def preprocess(self, text: str) -> Dict[str, torch.Tensor]:\n",
        "        cleaned_text = \" \".join(text.strip().split())\n",
        "        encoded = self.tokenizer(\n",
        "            cleaned_text,\n",
        "            padding=True,\n",
        "            truncation=True,\n",
        "            max_length=self.max_length,\n",
        "            return_tensors=\"pt\"\n",
        "        )\n",
        "        return {key: value.to(self.device) for key, value in encoded.items()}\n",
        "\n",
        "    def predict(self, text: str) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n",
        "        inputs = self.preprocess(text)\n",
        "        with torch.no_grad():\n",
        "            outputs = self.model(**inputs)\n",
        "\n",
        "        probabilities = torch.softmax(outputs.logits, dim=-1)[0]\n",
        "        predicted_index = int(torch.argmax(probabilities).item())\n",
        "        label = self.model.config.id2label[predicted_index]\n",
        "\n",
        "        return {\n",
        "            \"label\": label,\n",
        "            \"score\": float(probabilities[predicted_index].item()),\n",
        "            \"probabilities\": {\n",
        "                self.model.config.id2label[i]: float(probabilities[i].item())\n",
        "                for i in range(len(probabilities))\n",
        "            }\n",
        "        }\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "f0d7cc82",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "f0d7cc82"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "analyzer = BERTSentimentAnalyzer()\n",
        "samples = [\n",
        "    \"The workshop was clear, useful, and surprisingly fun.\",\n",
        "    \"The app kept crashing, and the instructions were confusing.\",\n",
        "    \"The lecture was fine, but I wanted more examples.\"\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "for text in samples:\n",
        "    print(text)\n",
        "    print(analyzer.predict(text))\n",
        "    print()\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "10669267",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "10669267"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Exercise 4 - BERT for Named Entity Recognition\n",
        "Objective: Build a lightweight class that runs a token-classification model and maps tokens to entity labels.\n",
        "\n",
        "Instructions:\n",
        "1. Import `AutoTokenizer` and `AutoModelForTokenClassification`.\n",
        "2. Implement `BERTNamedEntityRecognizer` with init plus a `recognize` method.\n",
        "3. Tokenize sample text, run the model, convert the predictions to entity spans, and test with a short paragraph.\n",
        "\n",
        "Deliverables:\n",
        "- The `recognize` method returns a list of dictionaries like `{text, entity, start, end}` for each detected entity.\n",
        "- Subword tokens are handled with tokenizer offset mappings, so merged entities are reconstructed from the original text rather than from raw WordPiece strings.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "6aa24c9a",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "6aa24c9a"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "from transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForTokenClassification\n",
        "import torch\n",
        "\n",
        "class BERTNamedEntityRecognizer:\n",
        "    def __init__(self, model_name: str = \"dslim/bert-base-NER\"):\n",
        "        self.model_name = model_name\n",
        "        self.device = torch.device(\"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\")\n",
        "        self.tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_name)\n",
        "        self.model = AutoModelForTokenClassification.from_pretrained(model_name).to(self.device)\n",
        "        self.model.eval()\n",
        "\n",
        "    def recognize(self, text: str):\n",
        "        encoded = self.tokenizer(\n",
        "            text,\n",
        "            return_offsets_mapping=True,\n",
        "            return_tensors=\"pt\",\n",
        "            truncation=True\n",
        "        )\n",
        "        offsets = encoded.pop(\"offset_mapping\")[0].tolist()\n",
        "        model_inputs = {key: value.to(self.device) for key, value in encoded.items()}\n",
        "\n",
        "        with torch.no_grad():\n",
        "            outputs = self.model(**model_inputs)\n",
        "\n",
        "        predictions = torch.argmax(outputs.logits, dim=-1)[0].cpu().tolist()\n",
        "        entities = []\n",
        "        current_entity = None\n",
        "\n",
        "        def close_current_entity():\n",
        "            nonlocal current_entity\n",
        "            if current_entity is not None:\n",
        "                current_entity[\"text\"] = text[current_entity[\"start\"]:current_entity[\"end\"]]\n",
        "                entities.append(current_entity)\n",
        "                current_entity = None\n",
        "\n",
        "        for prediction_id, (start, end) in zip(predictions, offsets):\n",
        "            if start == end:\n",
        "                continue\n",
        "\n",
        "            label = self.model.config.id2label[prediction_id]\n",
        "            if label == \"O\":\n",
        "                close_current_entity()\n",
        "                continue\n",
        "\n",
        "            prefix, entity_type = label.split(\"-\", 1)\n",
        "            should_start_new = (\n",
        "                prefix == \"B\"\n",
        "                or current_entity is None\n",
        "                or current_entity[\"entity\"] != entity_type\n",
        "                or start > current_entity[\"end\"] + 1\n",
        "            )\n",
        "\n",
        "            if should_start_new:\n",
        "                close_current_entity()\n",
        "                current_entity = {\"text\": \"\", \"entity\": entity_type, \"start\": start, \"end\": end}\n",
        "            else:\n",
        "                current_entity[\"end\"] = end\n",
        "\n",
        "        close_current_entity()\n",
        "        return entities\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "ddabab0a",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ddabab0a"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "ner = BERTNamedEntityRecognizer()\n",
        "sample_text = \"Sundar Pichai met OpenAI researchers in San Francisco before visiting Google in Mountain View.\"\n",
        "ner.recognize(sample_text)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "422bc57b",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "422bc57b"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Exercise 4 reflection\n",
        "The recognizer uses the model's BIO labels to decide when an entity begins, continues, or ends. WordPiece fragments such as tokens that start with `##` are not joined manually; instead, the tokenizer's `offset_mapping` provides character positions in the original sentence, and each merged entity is rebuilt by slicing the original text from `start` to `end`. This preserves spacing and removes WordPiece artifacts.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "ae59c960",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ae59c960"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Exercise 5 - Comparing BERT and GPT\n",
        "Objective: Summarize how encoder-style models differ from decoder-style models.\n",
        "\n",
        "Fill the table with concise statements (one line each).\n",
        "\n",
        "| Category | BERT | GPT |\n",
        "|----------|------|-----|\n",
        "| Architecture | Encoder-only Transformer trained with bidirectional attention. | Decoder-only Transformer trained with causal, left-to-right attention. |\n",
        "| Primary purpose | Understanding and representing text. | Generating and continuing text. |\n",
        "| Typical use cases | Classification, sentiment analysis, search embeddings, NER, question answering over a passage. | Chatbots, summarization, drafting, code generation, creative writing, tool-using assistants. |\n",
        "| Strengths | Strong context understanding because every token can attend to tokens on both sides. Efficient for labeling, ranking, and retrieval tasks. | Strong fluent generation and flexible instruction following. Can produce long, coherent responses from prompts. |\n",
        "| Weaknesses | Not designed to generate long free-form text by itself. Usually needs task-specific fine-tuning or a task head. | Less naturally suited to token-level labeling and bidirectional representation tasks. Can generate plausible text that is not grounded unless retrieval or checks are added. |\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "58dc9313",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "58dc9313"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Exercise 6 - BERT inside Retrieval-Augmented Generation\n",
        "Objective: Explain how BERT-generated embeddings power the retrieval stage of a RAG workflow.\n",
        "\n",
        "Address each bullet with a short paragraph:\n",
        "1. BERT can encode a query and each document passage into dense vectors that capture semantic meaning. In practice, teams often use BERT-style sentence embedding models so similar questions and passages land near each other in vector space even when they do not use the exact same words.\n",
        "2. The document embeddings are stored in a vector database such as FAISS, Pinecone, Weaviate, or Chroma. When a user asks a question, the system embeds the query, searches for the nearest document vectors with a similarity metric such as cosine similarity, and returns the most relevant passages.\n",
        "3. The retrieved passages are inserted into the prompt for a generative model like GPT. GPT then uses the supplied context to write an answer that is grounded in the retrieved information instead of relying only on its internal training data.\n",
        "4. A useful example is a customer-support assistant for a software company. BERT-style retrieval can find the most relevant help-center articles, release notes, and troubleshooting guides, while GPT turns those retrieved passages into a clear answer for the customer.\n"
      ]
    }
  ],
